6e58ee30b8339573.text:
I worked through the seven rules in order of the decision: time left, the required rate and pressure, the staff needed, the desired staff and the hiring pipeline.

{
 "criticisms": [
  {
   "element": "Schedule pressure",
   "quote": "Required_rate / MAX(Normal_capacity, 0.001)",
   "kind": "information",
   "why": "Normal capacity is built from Effective staff: headcount after training drain, the rookies' slower pace and communication overhead. Managers see the headcount, not this effective capacity, which they could only infer from throughput over several weeks. The rule gives them perfect, instant knowledge of their own team's productivity.",
   "test": {
    "equation": "Required_rate / MAX(Total_staff * Normal_productivity * (1 - QA_share), 0.001)"
   },
   "prediction": "Pressure judged on headcount is lower once new staff arrive, so less overtime and haste late in the project. Expect a slightly later finish, but less rework and less effort."
  },
  {
   "element": "Schedule pressure",
   "quote": "0.001",
   "kind": "constant",
   "why": "An unnamed number inside the equation. It is a guard against dividing by zero when nobody is on the project, not a parameter of behaviour, but it should be named or commented so that nobody calibrates it.",
   "test": null,
   "prediction": "No effect while capacity is positive."
  },
  {
   "element": "Staff needed",
   "quote": "Time_left * Normal_productivity",
   "kind": "other",
   "why": "Managers size the team on the nominal 1 task per person-week. Each person actually delivers less, because Normal capacity also takes off QA time and, through Effective staff, coordination, training and the slower pace of new staff. So the rule asks for too few people, and asks for them late.",
   "test": {
    "equation": "Work_to_do / (Time_left * Normal_capacity / MAX(Total_staff, 0.001))"
   },
   "prediction": "Planning on the productivity actually achieved asks for more staff earlier. Expect an earlier finish and more effort."
  },
  {
   "element": "Time left",
   "quote": "MAX(Deadline - TIME, Minimum_time_left)",
   "kind": "constant",
   "why": "Minimum time left is 2 weeks with no stated source. Close to and after the deadline it means managers plan to finish all the visible work within 2 weeks, so Staff needed jumps to half the work to do, and the hiring rule chases a target nobody would set.",
   "test": {
    "parameter": "Minimum time left",
    "values": [
     2,
     4,
     8
    ]
   },
   "prediction": "A longer floor caps staff needed near the deadline: less late hiring and less effort, with the finish week changing little."
  },
  {
   "element": "Desired staff",
   "quote": "MAX(0, Staff_needed - Initial_staff)",
   "kind": "units",
   "why": "The units balance (people minus people), but the meanings differ: Staff needed counts people at full productivity, while Initial staff and the stock it feeds count heads, rookies included. A new hire is counted as a whole person the day the post is opened.",
   "test": null,
   "prediction": "Tends to under-hire while many staff are new."
  },
  {
   "element": "Hiring",
   "quote": "Staff_needed / Time_to_fill_posts",
   "kind": "information",
   "why": "Hiring reads Staff needed directly, so it ignores the posts already open and keeps hiring for posts that are already being filled: the supply line is not counted, the classic cause of overshoot in stock management.",
   "test": null,
   "prediction": "Overshoot of staff above Desired staff."
  }
 ]
}
